## What to check

**Is the collision risk real.** Check the responsibility catalog
above and ask whether this term actually appears, or is likely to
appear, in more than one document where a drifted or duplicated name
would cause real confusion. A term with an unambiguous plain-English
reading, or one that's only ever going to be used in one place, is a
dictionary entry, not a vocab entry — flag it as disambiguation work
nobody needed done.

**Does the definition actually settle things.** Read `<definition>`
and ask whether it would end a naming argument between two engineers,
not just describe the term to someone unfamiliar with it. A definition
that restates the term in different words, or describes what the
concept is for without saying precisely what does and doesn't count as
an instance of it, is a finding — say what borderline case it fails to
resolve.

**Do the aliases earn their place.** Each `<alias>` should be a name
someone working from a different document would plausibly reach for.
An empty alias list when obvious near-synonyms exist in the catalog is
a gap worth flagging; a list padded with implausible variants is
noise but not blocking on its own — a missed real collision is.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and
one `<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each
`<location>` naming `definition` or the specific `alias` at fault, and
each `<comment>` saying what's wrong and what a passing version would
contain.
